The doctor takes his stethoscope.
He listens to my chest.
Then he takes a small light and looks into my throat.
"Your throat is very red," the doctor says.
He sits back down at his desk.
"You have a severe cold, Frau Flores," he says.
"The weather in April is often a problem, because it changes so quickly."
He looks at me seriously.
"You should sleep a lot now," he says.
"And you should drink a lot of warm tea."
He shakes his head.
"And no, you absolutely should not play badminton tomorrow."
"You need rest, because you are really sick."
He types something on his computer.
The small printer on the desk prints a prescription.
The prescription is green.
A green prescription means that I pay for the medicine myself.
The doctor gives me the green paper.
"Go to the pharmacy and then directly to bed," he says.
I thank him and stand up slowly.
